=== the attribute as the caller passes it ===

  970 characters
  From: dana.okafor@halloway-group.example — our API key sk-live-9f2b71c4ae8d55 stopped working af…

=== the attribute as it reaches the span ===

  414 characters
  From: [redacted:email] — our API key [redacted:secret] stopped working after the migration. Esca…
  …s 33.1%. We shipped 102,433 unit… [+547 chars]

  removed:
    the email address   gone
    the API key         gone
    the document body   truncated at 400 characters (556 characters dropped)

=== one retrieval, measured ===

  passages returned to the model   2,121 characters
  characters stored on spans       103
  ratio                            21x smaller

The span records that six passages came back and how many characters they
were. It does not record the passages. That is the whole rule, and it is not
about privacy alone — a trace carrying its own retrieved context is a copy of
your corpus, shipped to a third party, kept for ninety days, readable by
everyone with a dashboard login.

Three things that belong on every model span, and nothing else:

  identity     model, tenant, request id, prompt version
  shape        token counts, result counts, latency, cost
  decisions    which tools were chosen, whether it answered or refused

Three that never do:

  the prompt and the completion, in full
  retrieved documents, rows, or any user content
  anything that arrived in a header

If you need the text to debug — and sometimes you do — put it somewhere with
its own retention and its own access list, and put the *pointer* on the span.
§24.3 builds that, because reproducing a bad run needs the input and the trace
is the wrong place to keep it.
